=item type - integer. the type of sensor to query.

=item data - reference to a scalar holding number, pointer object or undef. a pointer filled with the current sensor state.

=item num_values - integer. the number of values to write to data.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_RumbleGamepad

  my $result = SDL_RumbleGamepad($gamepad, $low_frequency_rumble, $high_frequency_rumble, $duration_ms);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_RumbleGamepad(SDL_Gamepad * gamepad, Uint16 low_frequency_rumble, Uint16 high_frequency_rumble, Uint32 duration_ms) >>

Start a rumble effect on a gamepad.

Each call to this function cancels any previous rumble effect, and calling it with 0 intensity stops any rumbling.

This function requires you to process SDL events or call SDL_UpdateJoysticks() to update rumble state.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

=over

=item gamepad - SDL3::Gamepad object or undef. the gamepad to vibrate.

=item low_frequency_rumble - unsigned integer. the intensity of the low frequency (left) rumble motor, from 0 to 0xFFFF.

=item high_frequency_rumble - unsigned integer. the intensity of the high frequency (right) rumble motor, from 0 to 0xFFFF.

=item duration_ms - unsigned integer. the duration of the rumble effect, in milliseconds.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_RumbleGamepadTriggers

  my $result = SDL_RumbleGamepadTriggers($gamepad, $left_rumble, $right_rumble, $duration_ms);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_RumbleGamepadTriggers(SDL_Gamepad * gamepad, Uint16 left_rumble, Uint16 right_rumble, Uint32 duration_ms) >>

Start a rumble effect in the gamepad's triggers.

Each call to this function cancels any previous trigger rumble effect, and calling it with 0 intensity stops any rumbling.

Note that this is rumbling of the _triggers_ and not the gamepad as a whole. This is currently only supported on Xbox One gamepads. If you want the (more common) whole-gamepad rumble, use SDL_RumbleGamepad() instead.

This function requires you to process SDL events or call SDL_UpdateJoysticks() to update rumble state.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

=over

=item gamepad - SDL3::Gamepad object or undef. the gamepad to vibrate.

=item left_rumble - unsigned integer. the intensity of the left trigger rumble motor, from 0 to 0xFFFF.

=item right_rumble - unsigned integer. the intensity of the right trigger rumble motor, from 0 to 0xFFFF.

=item duration_ms - unsigned integer. the duration of the rumble effect, in milliseconds.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_SetGamepadLED

  my $result = SDL_SetGamepadLED($gamepad, $red, $green, $blue);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_SetGamepadLED(SDL_Gamepad * gamepad, Uint8 red, Uint8 green, Uint8 blue) >>

Update a gamepad's LED color.

An example of a joystick LED is the light on the back of a PlayStation 4's DualShock 4 controller.

For gamepads with a single color LED, the maximum of the RGB values will be used as the LED brightness.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

=over

=item gamepad - SDL3::Gamepad object or undef. the gamepad to update.

=item red - unsigned integer. the intensity of the red LED.

=item green - unsigned integer. the intensity of the green LED.

=item blue - unsigned integer. the intensity of the blue LED.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_SendGamepadEffect

  my $result = SDL_SendGamepadEffect($gamepad, $data, $size);

C: C<< _Bool SDL_SendGamepadEffect(SDL_Gamepad * gamepad, const void * data, int size) >>

Send a gamepad specific effect packet.

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

=over

=item gamepad - SDL3::Gamepad object or undef. the gamepad to affect.

=item data - SDL3::Pointer, object, string buffer or undef. the data to send to the gamepad.

=item size - integer. the size of the data to send to the gamepad.

=back

Returns boolean: true on success or false on failure; call SDL_GetError() for more information.

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_CloseGamepad

  SDL_CloseGamepad($gamepad);

C: C<< void SDL_CloseGamepad(SDL_Gamepad * gamepad) >>

Close a gamepad previously opened with SDL_OpenGamepad().

It is safe to call this function from any thread.

=over

=item gamepad - SDL3::Gamepad object or undef. a gamepad identifier previously returned by SDL_OpenGamepad().

=back

Since: This function is available since SDL 3.2.0.

=head2 SDL_GetGamepadAppleSFSymbolsNameForButton

  my $result = SDL_GetGamepadAppleSFSymbolsNameForButton($gamepad, $button);

